(project borge-docs-literate
 (section comment-integrity
  (doc "Protect human-authored comments from silent agent deletion")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Annotated comments with a human author (e.g. (* roerick note ... *))
        are first-class spec content. Agents must never delete them silently.

        For planning agents (borge plan): the agent edits .borg files in
        .borge.lock/working/. At commit time, borge checks whether any
        human-authored comments were deleted by comparing working/ with
        original/. If deletions are found, the commit is blocked. The
        user must either restore the deleted comments or run borge commit
        --force to override. The diff original/..working/ is shown.

        For implementing/fixing agents (borge make, borge drift --fix):
        these agents cannot modify .borg files, so this check does not
        apply. Code file comment integrity may be added in the future.

        borge lint (legacy) compares the working tree against git HEAD.
        If a human-authored comment is present in HEAD but missing in the
        working tree, lint emits a deletion warning. Nested comments are
        now captured by the parser and inspected by lint, so this no
        longer has the historical blind spot inside (project ...) or
        (section ...) forms. This still works for non-agent editing but
        is redundant when the agent workflow is used.

        borge normalize is the human saying "I made changes on purpose."
        It runs borge fmt, then accepts all comment deletions as
        intentional by recording the current state as the new baseline.
        After normalize, lint will not warn about those deletions again.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section code-documentation
  (doc "Crawl .ml files for documentation coverage under ocaml-dune convention")
  (status in-progress)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Under the ocaml-dune convention, borge drift checks code files
        too. For every .ml file it finds all let bindings (functions
        and values). A binding is documented if it has a borge-style
        annotated comment above it:
          (* author note (|description of what this does|) *)

        Undocumented bindings are reported as a coverage gap. This makes
        literate programming the default: functions should explain
        themselves. To exempt a binding from documentation:
          (* exempt doc *)

        The drift report shows: total bindings, documented count,
        undocumented count, coverage percentage. This is code drift
        by another name: the code exists but the spec (in the form of
        inline documentation) does not cover it.
      |))
  )
  (subsection doc-coverage-command
   (doc "borge drift --docs: analyze documentation coverage")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       Implementation: borge drift --docs flag triggers documentation
       coverage analysis.

       Algorithm (implemented in lib/doc/):
       1. Find all .ml files (respecting _build/.git exclusion)
          via Doc_extract.find_ml_files
       2. Extract all top-level let bindings via
          Doc_extract.extract_bindings
       3. For each binding, check for preceding doc comment via
          Doc_detect.extract_file_docs:
          - (** docstring *) (ocamldoc style)
          - (* author note (|...|) *) borg-style comment
          - (*| ... |*) alternative borg format
          - (* exempt doc *) — marks as exempt, not undocumented
       4. Skip bindings (handled by Doc_extract classification):
          - let%test (test bindings)
          - let _ = (ignored bindings)
          - Bindings starting with _ (implicitly private)
       5. Output: (documentation ...) block in .borg.meta
          via Doc_coverage.generate_meta_block

       Coverage calculation (Doc_coverage.calculate_file_coverage):
       - documented = count of bindings with valid doc
       - exempt = count of bindings with (* exempt doc *)
       - total-bindings = all extracted bindings (after filtering)
       - undocumented = total - documented - exempt
       - coverage-percent = documented / (total - exempt) * 100

       Drift findings produced:
       - undocumented-module: file with coverage < threshold
       - undocumented-function: specific binding missing doc (if --verbose)

       CLI output (non-json):
       lib/borge/spec.ml: 38/45 documented (84.4%), 2 exempt, 5 undocumented
       lib/borge/parse.ml: 12/28 documented (42.9%) ⚠ undocumented-module

       Integration with stats: borge stats aggregates all (documentation ...)
       blocks from .borg.meta files for project-wide coverage report.

       Remaining work:
       - Wire Doc_coverage into borge_drift.ml as --docs flag
       - Add --verbose flag for per-binding undocumented names
       - Add Cmdliner argument for threshold override
     |))
   )
  )
  (subsection doc-formats
   (doc "Supported documentation comment formats")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       OCaml supports multiple doc comment styles. Borge recognizes:

       1. Standard docstring (ocamldoc style):
          (** Convert a string to a status value.
              @raise Invalid_argument if string is unknown *)
          let status_of_string s = ...

       2. Borg-style annotated comment:
          (* roerick note (|
             This function implements the status conversion logic.
             It handles all valid status strings defined in the spec.
           |) *)
          let status_of_string s = ...

       3. Borg-style annotated comment with literacy score:
          (* agent note [5250] (|
             The balance checker is a paranoid paren counter. We do
             NOT trust the parser for structural validation because
             a syntax error in the parser itself could hide a real
             bug. So balance is a separate, dumber implementation.

             This is defense in depth: the parser and balancer are
             independent. They should agree. When they don't, at
             least one is wrong.
           |) *)
          let check_balance input = ...

       4. Short borg comment:
          (*| Convert string to status. Raises on invalid input. |*)
          let status_of_string s = ...

       5. Exemption marker:
          (* exempt doc *)
          let internal_helper x = ...

       The literacy score [NNNN] is optional on borg-style comments.
       See the comment-writing-guide subsection for the full
       scoring system.

       All formats are extracted during drift --docs analysis and
       contribute to coverage calculations.
     |))
   )
  )
  (subsection drift-doc-integration
   (doc "How doc coverage is reported in drift and enforced")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       When borge drift --docs runs, findings are generated in two
       places:

       1. .borg.meta files get (documentation ...) blocks with full
          metrics. These are machine-readable and git-tracked.

       2. Drift findings list includes coverage-related findings:
          - (finding undocumented-module (file PATH) (coverage PERCENT))
          - (finding undocumented-function (file PATH) (name NAME))
            (only with --verbose flag)

       Thresholds and enforcement:
       - Coverage >= threshold: no finding (good)
       - Coverage 50%-threshold: warning-level finding
       - Coverage < 50%: error-level finding
       - Default threshold: 80% (0.8)

       Exit codes:
       - 0: all files meet threshold or above
       - 1: at least one file below 50% (error-level)
       - 2: at least one file between 50% and threshold (warning-level)
         only when --strict flag is passed; otherwise exit 0

       --strict flag:
       Without --strict, warnings are printed but do not cause a
       non-zero exit. With --strict, any file below the threshold
       (including warning-level) causes a non-zero exit. This allows
       CI to gradually enforce coverage: start without --strict,
       fix the errors, then add --strict to catch warnings too.

       The threshold is configurable per-project in borge.borg:
       (convention ocaml-dune
         (doc-coverage-threshold 0.7))

       Remaining work:
       - Wire Doc_coverage.meets_threshold into drift exit code logic
       - Add --strict and --verbose Cmdliner args to borge_drift.ml
       - Read doc-coverage-threshold from borge.borg convention stanza
     |))
   )
  )
  (subsection comment-writing-guide
   (doc "How to write code comments — literacy scoring for agents and humans")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       Comments are not checkboxes. They are how the codebase tells
       its story. The goal is not coverage — it is literacy. Can
       someone read this file like a book and understand it?

       Every borg-style comment carries a 4-digit literacy score:

         (* agent note [STORY EXPLAIN TEACH EDGE] (| ... |) *)

       Each digit is 0–7, self-declared by the author based on
       what the comment actually contains. Zeros are honest and
       common — most comments do not need to excel at everything.

       1. STORY — Does this comment connect to a narrative?
          0: No story. Just facts about this function.
          5: Has context. Explains why this exists in the story
             of the module — what came before, what comes after,
             why you're reading it now.
          7: Reads like a book chapter. You'd publish it. Someone
             could understand the module's architecture from the
             comments alone, without reading code.

       2. EXPLAIN — Does it explain what's happening in the code?
          0: Doesn't explain the implementation.
          5: Explains the algorithm or data flow. Someone could
             understand how the code works without reading every
             line.
          7: Walks you through it like a debugger session. Line
             by line where needed. Reveals the mechanics.

       3. TEACH — Does it teach something transferable?
          0: No transferable insight.
          5: Teaches a technique, pattern, or gotcha — something
             you'll use elsewhere in the codebase or in other
             projects.
          7: Teaches a fundamental concept. You'll remember it
             years later in unrelated code.

       4. EDGE — Does it say what breaks?
          0: No boundary discussion. Only the happy path.
          5: Major edge cases, preconditions, or failure modes
             mentioned. You know at least one thing that can go
             wrong.
          7: Every precondition stated, invariants explicit,
             failure modes catalogued. Reads like an incident
             report written by someone who got paged at 3am.

       Examples:

       A solid everyday comment — doesn't need to be a dissertation:
         (* agent note [5200] (|
            The balance checker is a paranoid paren counter. We
            do NOT trust the parser for structural validation
            because a parser bug could hide a real structural
            error. So balance is a separate, dumber implementation.

            This is defense in depth — two independent checks
            that should agree.
          |) *)

       A wrapper that needs almost no comment — honest zeros:
         (* agent note [5000] (|
            WRAPPER: balance-verbose is the same scanner as
            balance but with a paren stack for error reporting.
            Same algorithm, more memory.
          |) *)

       An edge-case-heavy function that needs thorough warning:
         (* agent note [5557] (|
            Extracts named elements from a UI element tree.

            Edge cases handled:
            - Empty tree: returns empty list (not an error)
            - Duplicate names: all occurrences collected, caller
              deduplicates if needed
            - Deep nesting: recurses through all children, O(depth)
            - Null names: elements without names are silently
              skipped (they're structural, not semantic)

            Invariant: returned list preserves document order.
            Do not sort it — downstream tools expect FIFO.
          |) *)

       Guidelines:
       - Score honestly. [5000] is not a failing grade — it means
         "I told the story but didn't explain mechanics, teach
         principles, or catalogue edge cases." That's fine for
         many functions.
       - Zeros are the default. Adding a dimension takes effort.
         If you didn't put edge cases in the comment, score EDGE
         as 0. Don't inflate scores to look good.
       - borge lint checks presence: every agent note must have a
         [NNNN] score block. Missing score is a lint warning.
       - borge lint checks plausibility: if you claim [7] on
         TEACH but the comment contains no transferable insight,
         lint suggests adjusting the score or adding content.
       - When a comment changes, reset the 4th digit (EDGE)
         and 1st digit (STORY if module context changed) to
         reflect unverified currency. Humans raise digits; agents
         should not claim currency they haven't verified.
       - Exempt internal helpers with (* exempt doc *) only if
         the name is truly self-documenting.
     |))
   )
  )
  (subsection code-doc-drift-status
   (doc "Mark inline code comments as drifted from implementation")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       When a code comment no longer accurately describes its
       function, it should be marked as drifted. This signals to
       agents and humans that the comment needs updating — it is
       not a silent lie, it is an explicit flag.

       Syntax: add (status drifted) to the borg-comment:
         (* agent note (|
            WHAT: Parse a .borg file into a typed AST.

            WHY: Downstream tools need structured access.
          |) (status drifted) *)
         let parse_file input = ...

       Or in the short borg format:
         (*| Parse .borg into typed AST. | (status drifted) *)

       Or in standard docstring:
         (** Parse a .borg file into a typed AST.
             @drifted comment does not mention error handling *)

       When to mark drifted:
       - An agent modifies a function's behavior but doesn't
         update the comment (agent should mark it, not ignore it)
       - A human changes code and notices the comment is stale
       - borge review detects doc-code mismatch and marks it

       When NOT to mark drifted:
       - Just update the comment instead. Drifted is for cases
         where you can't or won't fix it right now — you're
         acknowledging the gap, not endorsing it.

       Drift status in borg-comments:
       The (status drifted) form inside a borg-comment is
       analogous to (status drifted) in a .borg section. Both
       say: this description once matched reality, but no longer.

       Integration with borge review:
       - borge review checks for (status drifted) markers
       - If found, the finding is downgraded from "undocumented"
         to "drifted" — the function has a comment, but it's
         known-stale. This is better than undocumented (something
         was there) but worse than documented (it's accurate).
       - Coverage calculation: drifted comments count as
         documented for the numerator but are flagged separately
         in the report.

       Integration with borge lint:
       - (status drifted) markers produce a Stale_doc_comment
         lint warning (not error — the gap is acknowledged).
       - Without the marker, a stale comment that doesn't match
         the code is an unacknowledged lie — that should be
         caught by borge review (agentic), not lint (static).
     |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section code-comment-integrity
  (doc "Lint .ml files for missing documentation on exported bindings")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The existing comment-integrity section protects .borg file
        comments from silent agent deletion. This section extends
        lint to .ml code files, enforcing the literate-programming
        rule that every exported function must have a doc comment.

        This is the enforcement arm of code-documentation:
        drift --docs detects and reports coverage gaps; lint
        --code-doc makes it a pass/fail check.

        New lint issue types for .ml files:
        - Undocumented_binding: exported binding at PATH:LINE with
          name NAME has no preceding doc comment and no
          (* exempt doc *) marker. Severity: warning by default,
          error with --strict.
        - Stale_doc_comment: a doc comment exists but the binding
          signature below it has changed since the doc was last
          reviewed (future: compare against .borg.meta reviewed-at
          timestamp). Severity: warning.

        Integration with existing lint pipeline:
        - borge lint already walks .borg files. This adds a second
          pass over .ml files using Doc_extract.extract_bindings and
          Doc_detect.extract_file_docs.
        - New lint_issue variant:
          | Undocumented_binding of { path; name; line }
          | Stale_doc_comment of { path; name; line; last_reviewed }
        - These are included in lint_result.issues alongside existing
          .borg issues.
        - Error/warning classification follows drift-doc-integration
          thresholds: undocumented on exported binding = warning,
          undocumented with --strict = error.

        Exemptions:
        - Bindings starting with _ are internal, not checked.
        - (* exempt doc *) marker skips the binding.
        - Test bindings (let%) are skipped.
        - .mli-only modules: the .mli is the surface, but doc comments
          on .ml bindings are still expected. A binding with
          no .ml doc but a .mli doc is still flagged — the doc
          should live with the implementation.

        CLI:
        - borge lint (existing): now also checks .ml files when
          the code-comment-integrity section is present and not
          (status planned).
        - borge lint --code-doc: explicitly enable .ml file checks
          even if section status is planned (for gradual adoption).
        - No separate subcommand — this integrates into the existing
          lint flow.

        Exit code impact:
        - Undocumented_binding counts as warning in lint_result.
        - With --strict, counts as error.
        - lint already exits 1 if error_count > 0, 0 otherwise.

        Future:
        - Stale_doc_comment needs .borg.meta reviewed-at tracking.
        - Could integrate with semantic review: if borge review
          finds low doc-accuracy, that could promote the lint issue
          from warning to error.
      |))
  )
  (subsection lint-code-doc-flag
   (doc "borge lint --code-doc: enable .ml file doc checks")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
       Adds a Cmdliner --code-doc flag to borge_lint.ml.

       When passed (or when code-comment-integrity status is not
       planned), the lint runner does a second pass:
       1. Find all .ml files via Doc_extract.find_ml_files
       2. For each file, extract bindings and docs
       3. For each exported binding without doc or exemption,
          emit Undocumented_binding lint issue
       4. Add to the existing lint_result and print alongside
          .borg issues

       Output format (consistent with existing lint):
         ⚠ lib/borge/spec.ml:42: undocumented binding 'parse_section'
         ⚠ lib/borge/drift.ml:15: undocumented binding 'check_code_drift'

       Or with --strict:
         ✗ lib/borge/spec.ml:42: undocumented binding 'parse_section'
     |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section ast-position-tracking
  (doc "Source position tracking on AST nodes for better diagnostics")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Currently only annotated comments have start_line/end_line. The
        sexp type itself carries no position info. Adding start_pos and
        end_pos to every sexp node would enable:
          - balance to report which ( at which line was closed early
          - nodes to show positions for each AST node
          - drift to map spec nodes to source locations
          - editors to jump to spec definitions
        The position type should include line, col, and offset to
        support both human-readable and machine-consumable output.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section literate-programming
  (doc "The borge philosophy: spec is the primary artifact, code is derived")
  (status implemented)
  (* agent note (|
     roerick, on the original goal (2026-05-28): "we were initially
     going to build out a feature set where we A. implement a policy of
     comments for every function in our analyzed code and B. implement
     a static analysis linter to make sure that each function has
     comments, a la literate programming. this way we can determine if
     functions are doing what they say, and better analyze drift."

     The ambition grew beyond mechanical coverage (2026-06-19):
     "I'm generally not thrilled with the amount and quality of
     comments in the code. they're nice. but they feel like a
     checkbox, rather than like a living book or document which i
     can read to understand the codebase... maybe a scoring metric
     to show 'how effectively does this comment tell our story'."
     That tension — between checkbox coverage and readable
     narrative — is the animating problem of this section.
   |) *)
  (details
   (doc (|
        In a borge project, the spec is not documentation — it IS the
        source of truth. The .borg file is the artifact. Borge reads
        specs, validates them, reports on them, and helps agents edit
        them. But borge does NOT generate code. Every .borg file is
        both a human-readable design document and a machine-checkable
        contract.

        The three pillars:

        1. Spec first: write (status planned) sections before code.
           Use borge make spec to turn todos into structured specs.

        2. Implement via agent: borge make opens an interactive session
           where an agent implements planned sections. The agent writes
           code AND updates the spec, journaling changes. This is NOT
           code generation — the spec is authored, not derived.

        3. Verify continuously: borge balance, check, drift, and review
           keep spec and code aligned. Drift means the implementation
           has diverged from the spec. Fix the code or update the spec.

        A completed project is one where every section is (status
        implemented), every file has a code review, and borge drift
        reports zero items.
      |))
  )
 )
)
